  "type": "Activity",
  "number": "2.8.11",
  "title": "",
  "body": "  What is the output from the following code?  a = 3 b = (a != 3) print(b)      True    This would be true if a was not equal to 3, but it is.      False    A is equal to three so this statement is false.      0    Some languages use 0 for false, but Python will print False.      3    This does not set b to the value of a.      Syntax error    This code is legal.     "
